"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+3 with tertiary pattern 4 (ISUP grade group 1) and pathological stage pT2c, with no extraprostatic extension and no seminal vesicle invasion. Positive surgical margins are a concerning factor. The absence of lymph nodes removed and the lack of lymphovascular invasion are favorable findings.\n\nCountervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 0.2. However, the lack of documented nodal sampling introduces uncertainty, as pNx is not equivalent to pN0. The difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, with the biopsy report lacking Gleason pattern and ISUP information. The postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are critical missing pieces of information that would significantly revise this assessment. The current heuristic horizon suggests a follow-up period of approximately 71 months without an event. The fixed estimated horizon is 70.80 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 42.48–99.12 months; its coverage has not been validated."
